[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Compiler-stability evidence
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The suite was also run against the C compiled at `-O0`, `-O1`, `-O2` and `-O3`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (out-of-tree, `c_src/` untouched): **0 failures at every level**, i.e. the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation is bit-faithful across GCC's optimisation pipeline. `-Ofast` does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m diverge, by 1–2 ULP in the witness points only — that flag enables
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `-ffast-math`, which abandons IEEE semantics and reassociates the float
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m expressions; it is not this project's build (`CMakeLists.txt` sets no `-O` flag).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## NaN payload caveat
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The one relaxation in the comparison helper (`tests/common/mod.rs::feq`) is that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m two NaNs compare equal regardless of sign bit / payload. Measured example:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `c2Dot((FLT_MAX, -inf), (-NaN, +NaN))` gives `0x7fc00000` from GCC and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0xffc00000` from rustc. Which operand's NaN survives `mulss` follows the x86
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m instruction's operand order, which the C expression `a.x*b.x + a.y*b.y` does not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fix. Everything else is strict: `-0.0 != 0.0`, `inf` must match exactly, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m NaN-vs-non-NaN is a failure.
